        "description": "<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"\" data-caption=\"Image: Angela Weiss / AFP via Getty Images | AFP via Getty Images\" data-portal-copyright=\"AFP via Getty Images\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/gettyimages-2294777879.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\tImage: Angela Weiss / AFP via Getty Images | AFP via Getty Images\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">New York City residents struggling to get out of recurring subscription fees can now submit complaints to the city government. As of Thursday, <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/policy/964165/nyc-is-resurrecting-a-court-stricken-click-to-cancel-rule\">the city's click-to-cancel rule</a> has taken effect, which requires businesses to make it as easy to cancel a subscription as it is to sign up. That means a gym that lets you join online, for example, can't make you call or go in person to end your subscription.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-bloc",
